{
  "id": "dsm-026",
  "pattern": "data-structure-misuse",
  "difficulty_rating": 1600,
  "explanation": "`dict.fromkeys(categories, [])` does not create a fresh empty list per key — it creates ONE list object and reuses that same reference as the value for every key. So `groups['fruit']` and `groups['veg']` are literally the same list, and every `.append()` call — regardless of which category key it went through — lands in that one shared list. All three items (2 fruit, 1 veg) end up counted under both categories, so summarize2 reports {'fruit': 3, 'veg': 3} instead of the intended {'fruit': 2, 'veg': 1}.",
  "prompt": "group_by_category is meant to give every category its own list of item names. Step through summarize2 and predict the counts it returns for two fruit items and one veg item.",
  "language": "python",
  "snippet": "def group_by_category(items, categories):\n    groups = dict.fromkeys(categories, [])\n    for item in items:\n        groups[item[\"category\"]].append(item[\"name\"])\n    return groups\n\ndef summarize2(items, categories):\n    groups = group_by_category(items, categories)\n    return {cat: len(names) for cat, names in groups.items()}\n\nitems = [\n    {\"category\": \"fruit\", \"name\": \"apple\"},\n    {\"category\": \"veg\", \"name\": \"carrot\"},\n    {\"category\": \"fruit\", \"name\": \"banana\"},\n]\nprint(summarize2(items, [\"fruit\", \"veg\"]))",
